  "grammarNotes": [
    {
      "pattern": "早期白话：罢＝吧，么＝吗，伊＝她，的＝地",
      "explanation": "Lu Xun wrote in the early vernacular of the 1920s, before today's standard forms settled. 罢 is the modern particle 吧, 么 at the end of a question is 吗, 伊 means 她 (she), and 的 is often used where modern Chinese writes adverbial 地 (远远的站着 = 远远地站着).",
      "exampleFromStory": "让我拿去罢。",
      "exampleFromStoryEn": "Let me take it.",
      "additionalExamples": [
        "“管贼么？”（＝管贼吗？）",
        "人都叫伊“豆腐西施”。（＝叫她）"
      ],
      "additionalExamplesEn": [
        "\"Guard them from thieves?\" (= 管贼吗?)",
        "Everyone called her the \"Beancurd Beauty.\" (= 叫她)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "pattern": "只是……罢了",
      "explanation": "\"It's only…, that's all.\" 罢了 at the end of a sentence plays something down, often with 只是, 不过 or 只 earlier in the sentence. Modern Chinese still uses it, especially in writing.",
      "exampleFromStory": "这只是我自己心情的改变罢了。",
      "exampleFromStoryEn": "It was only my own mood that had changed, that's all.",
      "additionalExamples": [
        "我只是随便问问罢了，你别多想。",
        "他不过是开个玩笑罢了。"
      ],
      "additionalExamplesEn": [
        "I was just asking, that's all — don't read anything into it.",
        "He was only joking, that's all."
      ]
    },
    {
      "pattern": "愈……便愈……（＝越……越……）",
      "explanation": "\"The more…, the more…\" 愈 is a written word for 越. Yang Ersao uses it twice in a row to make a sarcastic circular argument.",
      "exampleFromStory": "真是愈有钱，便愈是一毫不肯放松，愈是一毫不肯放松，便愈有钱……",
      "exampleFromStoryEn": "It's true: the richer people get, the less they'll part with a single cent, and the less they'll part with a single cent, the richer they get…",
      "additionalExamples": [
        "雨愈下愈大，我们只好在车站等着。",
        "这本书我愈读愈喜欢。"
      ],
      "additionalExamplesEn": [
        "The rain got heavier and heavier, so we had to wait at the station.",
        "The more I read this book, the more I like it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "pattern": "一面……一面……",
      "explanation": "Two actions happening at the same time: \"while…, (also)…\". It works like 一边……一边…… but sounds more written.",
      "exampleFromStory": "圆规一面愤愤的回转身，一面絮絮的说，慢慢向外走。",
      "exampleFromStoryEn": "The Compasses turned away indignantly, muttering on and on, and walked slowly out.",
      "additionalExamples": [
        "他一面听，一面在本子上记着。",
        "我们一面吃饭，一面聊着小时候的事。"
      ],
      "additionalExamplesEn": [
        "He listened while taking notes in his notebook.",
        "We ate and chatted about when we were little."
      ]
    },
    {
      "pattern": "仿佛……似的 / 像……似的",
      "explanation": "\"As if… / just like…\". 似的 closes a comparison that starts with 仿佛, 像 or 好像. It can also follow a noun directly: 闪电似的 \"like lightning\".",
      "exampleFromStory": "但又总觉得被什么挡着似的，单在脑里面回旋，吐不出口外去。",
      "exampleFromStoryEn": "But somehow it felt as if something was blocking it; the words just went round and round in my head and would not come out.",
      "additionalExamples": [
        "他高兴得像个孩子似的。",
        "她仿佛什么都没听见似的，继续往前走。"
      ],
      "additionalExamplesEn": [
        "He was as happy as a child.",
        "She kept walking as if she hadn't heard anything."
      ]
    },
    {
      "pattern": "无所谓……，无所谓……",
      "explanation": "In the story's final lines 无所谓有，无所谓无 means \"it cannot be said to exist, nor can it be said not to exist\". In everyday modern Chinese, 无所谓 usually means \"it doesn't matter / I don't mind\".",
      "exampleFromStory": "希望本是无所谓有，无所谓无的。",
      "exampleFromStoryEn": "Hope cannot be said to exist, nor can it be said not to exist.",
      "additionalExamples": [
        "去哪儿吃饭我都无所谓，你决定吧。",
        "成功和失败，对他来说似乎无所谓。"
      ],
      "additionalExamplesEn": [
        "I don't mind where we eat — you decide.",
        "Success or failure seems to make no difference to him."
      ]
    }
  ],
